s-module___wt83G__main\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L4\",null,{\"parallelRouterKey\":\"children\",\"error\":\"$undefined\",\"errorStyles\":\"$undefined\",\"errorScripts\":\"$undefined\",\"template\":[\"$\",\"$L5\",null,{}],\"templateStyles\":\"$undefined\",\"templateScripts\":\"$undefined\",\"notFound\":[[[\"$\",\"title\",null,{\"children\":\"404: This page could not be found.\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"style\":{\"fontFamily\":\"system-ui,\\\"Segoe UI\\\",Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif,\\\"Apple Color Emoji\\\",\\\"Segoe UI Emoji\\\"\",\"height\":\"100vh\",\"textAlign\":\"center\",\"display\":\"flex\",\"flexDirection\":\"column\",\"alignItems\":\"center\",\"justifyContent\":\"center\"},\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"style\",null,{\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"body{color:#000;background:#fff;margin:0}.next-error-h1{border-right:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.3)}@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){body{color:#fff;background:#000}.next-error-h1{border-right:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.3)}}\"}}],[\"$\",\"h1\",null,{\"className\":\"next-error-h1\",\"style\":{\"display\":\"inline-block\",\"margin\":\"0 20px 0 0\",\"padding\":\"0 23px 0 0\",\"fontSize\":24,\"fontWeight\":500,\"verticalAlign\":\"top\",\"lineHeight\":\"49px\"},\"children\":404}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"style\":{\"display\":\"inline-block\"},\"children\":[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"style\":{\"fontSize\":14,\"fontWeight\":400,\"lineHeight\":\"49px\",\"margin\":0},\"children\":\"This page could not be found.\"}]}]]}]}]],[]],\"forbidden\":\"$undefined\",\"unauthorized\":\"$undefined\"}],[\"$\",\"$L1c\",null,{}],[\"$\",\"$L1d\",null,{}],[\"$\",\"$L1e\",null,{}]]}]}]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"15:{}\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"1f:I[96222,[\"/_next/static/chunks/2bd45f40cc5d6573.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d04c481ca7424b28.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/b43d582805300480.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/20089cdc3431a20d.js\"],\"SiteHeader\"]\n20:I[96222,[\"/_next/static/chunks/2bd45f40cc5d6573.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d04c481ca7424b28.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/b43d582805300480.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/20089cdc3431a20d.js\"],\"PageAlert\"]\n21:I[4517,[\"/_next/static/chunks/2bd45f40cc5d6573.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d04c481ca7424b28.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/b43d582805300480.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/20089cdc3431a20d.js\"],\"Breadcrumbs\"]\n22:I[96222,[\"/_next/static/chunks/2bd45f40cc5d6573.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d04c481ca7424b28.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/b43d582805300480.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/20089cdc3431a20d.js\"],\"EventPageHeader\"]\n23:I[96222,[\"/_next/static/chunks/2bd45f40cc5d6573.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d04c481ca7424b28.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/b43d582805300480.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/20089cdc3431a20d.js\"],\"ChallengeCta\"]\n24:T25d0,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\u003cp data-block-key=\"8zkle\"\u003eSelecting future landing sites and lunar base locations depends on many factors, including topography, geology, environment, and available resources, to name a few. Places on Earth that have analogous characteristics to the Moon or Mars – the Atacama Desert, Haughton Crater, Death Valley – allow mission teams to test equipment and methods before they fly; however, such terrestrial analog sites are not entirely well characterized. Your challenge is to use open Earth, Moon, and Mars data from NASA and other space agencies to identify and characterize new terrestrial analogs for future bases or landing sites, drawing on deserts, polar regions, volcanic and arid terrains, caves, and other environments that share conditions with the lunar or Martian surface, then present your findings as a map, visualization, or other tool.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"1o7a\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\n    \u003ciframe data-wagtail-youtube width=\"200\" height=\"113\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/Qiz_Pny-xjo?feature=oembed\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen title=\"Challenge: Identify Earth Locations that Analog the Permanent Moon Base Locations and Mars\"\u003e\u003c/iframe\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cp data-block-key=\"b1g7a\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2 data-block-key=\"7fi8p\"\u003eBackground\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"j6k1\"\u003eEarth is our most accessible laboratory for preparing to explore the Moon and Mars. Scientists and engineers already use terrestrial “analog” environments — deserts, polar regions, volcanic landscapes, impact craters, and caves — to study planetary processes and test instruments, technologies, and exploration concepts. However, no location on Earth perfectly reproduces another planetary environment, and different locations may provide useful analogs for different characteristics.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"69gja\"\u003eAs humanity prepares for sustained exploration of the Moon and future human exploration of Mars, we could use open Earth, Moon, and Mars data to search globally and systematically for terrestrial environments that may provide useful — and perhaps previously unrecognized — analogs. Participants might consider dimensions such as geology and surface materials, terrain type and topography, atmospheric and aeolian processes such as dust storms, temperature and other environmental extremes, water or ice, biological and habitability characteristics, subsurface environments, and conditions relevant to robotic or human operations.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"6mkck\"\u003eThe goal is not simply to rediscover well-known analog sites, or to find one place that simply looks like the Moon or Mars. Participants are encouraged to combine datasets creatively to identify and characterize locations anywhere on Earth, explain what makes them analogous and what does not, and show how these environments could help conduct lunar or Mars science and exploration. Solutions might range from identifying a compelling individual site, environment, or geophysical process to developing maps, similarity metrics, machine learning approaches, or other innovative methods for discovering and comparing planetary analogs. You might consider Earth, Moon, Mars data obtained from various NASA and other space agencies missions, as well as data and images obtained locally.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2 data-block-key=\"3foh\"\u003eObjectives\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"2h2pj\"\u003eYour challenge is to search our own planet Earth for places, environments and processes that could serve as analogs and can help us explore the Moon and Mars. Using open NASA Earth data together with available Moon and Mars data from other space agencies and open sources, identify and characterize one or more locations on Earth that could serve as useful analogs for candidate permanent Moon-base locations or future Mars landing and exploration sites. Explore what makes your Earth location analogous to the Moon or Mars. You might compare data and images of geology and surface materials, terrain and topography, temperature and environmental extremes, water or ice, dust and atmospheric processes, biology and habitability, caves or subsurface environments, or conditions relevant to robotic and human exploration. Your analog does not need to match the Moon or Mars in every way. Explain which characteristics or processes provide a strong analogy, where the analogy breaks down, and how studying or using your Earth analog could help prepare for the exploration of the Moon or Mars.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"an3o4\"\u003eBe creative in how you search for, analyze, and communicate your analog. You could develop a written report, an interactive map, visualization, app, story, similarity-ranking system, ML tool, or another innovative approach. You might investigate a single compelling location, compare several locations around the world, or develop a method for systematically searching Earth for previously unrecognized analogs. Use open data from NASA and other space agencies as your window into Earth, the Moon, and Mars—and show us what our home planet can teach us about exploring the next ones. You might consider documenting your findings in a written format to share with other enthusiasts interested in the topic and to educate the public.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2 data-block-key=\"dmeap\"\u003ePotential Considerations\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"4k2hs\"\u003e\u003ci\u003eYou may (but are not required to) consider the following:\u003c/i\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cul\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"9p4af\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eThink beyond visual similarity.\u003c/b\u003e Two places may look alike but differ substantially in geology, environment, or physical processes. Use data to explain why your proposed Earth location is a meaningful analog.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"8g5n7\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eConsider different dimensions of analogy\u003c/b\u003e, including:\u003cul\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"el85g\"\u003eGeology, mineralogy, and surface materials\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"dt666\"\u003eTerrain, elevation, slope, roughness, craters, and other topographic features\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"bfu3c\"\u003eTemperature, illumination, and environmental extremes\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"3ndd7\"\u003eWater, ice, permafrost, and other potential resources\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"3ig8q\"\u003eDust, wind, erosion, and other atmospheric or surface processes\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"cn69r\"\u003eBiology, extremophiles, habitability, and biosignatures\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"9s7pa\"\u003eCaves, lava tubes, underground environments, and other subsurface features\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"4gv1o\"\u003eConditions relevant to landing, mobility, navigation, robotic operations, human exploration, or construction\u003c/li\u003e\u003c/ul\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"bc99k\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eAn analog does not have to match everything.\u003c/b\u003e An Earth location might be an excellent geological analog but a poor temperature analog, for example. Identify what is similar, how similar it is, and what is different.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"85up\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eThink about processes as well as places.\u003c/b\u003e Earth processes such as sedimentation, dust storms, volcanic activity, erosion, ice dynamics, or water-driven alteration may provide insights into processes occurring today — or that occurred in the past — on the Moon or Mars.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"dvrmt\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eLook beyond well-known analog sites.\u003c/b\u003e Locations such as Antarctica, the Atacama Desert, Iceland, Hawaii are already widely studied. For example, NASA's global Earth-observation datasets may allow you to discover compelling analogs in places that have received much less attention.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"f2i8a\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eExplore the whole Earth, including below its surface.\u003c/b\u003e Polar regions, deserts, mountains, volcanoes, impact craters, salt flats, glaciers, caves, lava tubes, mines, and other environments could all offer interesting possibilities.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"4uor4\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eUse multiple datasets when useful.\u003c/b\u003e Combining observations of terrain, temperature, geology, vegetation, water, ice, aerosols, or other characteristics may reveal relationships that are difficult to recognize using a single dataset.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"9qtg5\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eConsider scale.\u003c/b\u003e A continent or desert does not need to be treated as a single analog. A particular crater, valley, volcanic field, cave system, or even a small area within a much larger region could provide a much stronger match.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"t0f2\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eConnect your analog to exploration.\u003c/b\u003e Explain how the location or process you identify could help scientists and engineers understand Moon, Mars, or both; test instruments or technologies, prepare robotic or human operations; search for resources or signs of habitability; or reduce exploration risks.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"1sgot\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eBe creative with your approach.\u003c/b\u003e Maps and visualizations are welcome, but solutions could also use geographic information systems (GIS), statistical analysis, similarity metrics, machine learning, interactive applications, games, or other innovative methods.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"ev5cu\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eRemember Earth's limits as an analog.\u003c/b\u003e Earth cannot reproduce important lunar and Martian conditions such as reduced gravity, the lunar vacuum, the thin Martian atmosphere, or their radiation environments. Recognizing these differences is part of good scientific analysis.\u003c/li\u003e\u003cli data-block-key=\"c0kmn\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eSurprise us.\u003c/b\u003e A strong solution may identify an unexpected Earth location, reveal an unexpected connection between Earth and Moon or Mars, or introduce a new way of using Earth data to help enable future exploration.\u003c/li\u003e\u003c/ul\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"48lnm\"\u003e\u003ci\u003eFor data and resources related to this challenge, refer to the Resources tab at the top of the page. More resources may be added before the hackathon begins.\u003c/i\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"25:T1478,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\u003cp data-block-key=\"8k70b\"\u003e\u003ci\u003eNASA does not endorse any non-U.S. Government entity and is not responsible for information contained on non-U.S. Government websites. For non-U.S. Government websites, participants must comply with any data use parameters of that specific website.\u003c/i\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2 data-block-key=\"ccvva\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eNASA Data \u0026amp; Resources\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"6rhjv\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eEarth\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"9d7pv\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://search.earthdata.nasa.gov/?utm_source=%27chatgpt.com%27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"  \u003e\u003cb\u003eNASA Earthdata Search\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cb\u003e:\u003c/b\u003e NASA's primary portal for discovering and accessing Earth science observations.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"c77e8\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://worldview.earthdata.nasa.gov/index.html?utm_source=%27chatgpt.com%27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"  \u003e\u003cb\u003eNASA Worldview\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cb\u003e:\u003c/b\u003e An easy-to-use interactive environment for visually exploring NASA satellite imagery and environmental observations.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"7eugp\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eMoon and Mars\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"2gh49\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://pds.nasa.gov/?utm_source=%27chatgpt.com%27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"  \u003e\u003cb\u003eNASA Planetary Data System (PDS)\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cb\u003e:\u003c/b\u003e NASA's long-term archive for planetary science data from NASA orbital, lander, and rover missions. It provides access to lunar and Martian mission observations spanning imagery, geoscience, atmospheres, plasma environments, cartography, and other measurements.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"eicpb\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/lro/?utm_source=%27chatgpt.com%27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external \u003e\u003cb\u003eNASA Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter (LRO)\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cb\u003e:\u003c/b\u003e LRO provides extensive observations of the lunar surface through instruments including LROC, LOLA, Diviner, LEND, Mini-RF and others.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"4ps89\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://pds-geosciences.wustl.edu/missions/lro/diviner.htm?utm_source=%27chatgpt.com%27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external \u003e\u003cb\u003eNASA LRO Diviner Lunar Data\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cb\u003e:\u003c/b\u003e Diviner maps lunar surface temperatures and provides especially useful products for studying the lunar poles. Its polar and resource products could be compared with extreme cold, ice-bearing, or other terrestrial environments.\u003c/p\u003e\u003ch2 data-block-key=\"57go5\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eSpace Agency Partner Resources\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/h2\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"8dpn1\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eCSA (Canadian Space Agency)\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"eht8l\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://donnees-data.asc-csa.gc.ca/en/dataset/rcm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external \u003e\u003cb\u003eRADARSAT Constellation Mission (RCM) - Data\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cb\u003e:\u003c/b\u003e RCM is Canada's new generation of Earth observation satellites. The RCM includes a trio of Earth observation satellites, capable of scanning Earth day or night and in any weather conditions. The RCM is designed to provide effective solutions in three main areas: Maritime surveillance (ice, surface wind, oil pollution and ship monitoring); Disaster management (mitigation, warning, response and recovery); and Ecosystem monitoring (agriculture, wetlands, forestry and coastal change monitoring).\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"bkphs\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://donnees-data.asc-csa.gc.ca/en/dataset/radarsat1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external \u003e\u003cb\u003eRADARSAT 1 - Data\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cb\u003e:\u003c/b\u003e Launched in November 1995, RADARSAT-1 provided Canada and the world with an operational radar satellite system capable of timely delivery of large amounts of data. Equipped with a powerful synthetic aperture radar (SAR) instrument, it acquired images of the Earth day or night, in all weather and through cloud cover, smoke and haze. All data products in the Canadian RADARSAT-1 archive have been released under an open license.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"cfchm\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/asc-csa/Synthetic_Aperture_Radar_Tutorial\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external \u003e\u003cb\u003eSynthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) Python Tutorial\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cb\u003e:\u003c/b\u003e This tutorial demonstrates how to work with SAR imagery using Python and QGIS. It includes easy and practical steps for accessing and visualizing data.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"6unmk\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eESA (European Space Agency)\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"95oos\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/?utm_source=%27chatgpt.com%27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external \u003e\u003cb\u003eCopernicus Data Space Ecosystem\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cb\u003e:\u003c/b\u003e Provides free and open access to Sentinel Earth-observation data covering land, oceans, and atmosphere, with tools for visualization and processing.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"9c6so\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://opensciencedata.esa.int/?utm_source=%27chatgpt.com%27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external \u003e\u003cb\u003eESA Open Science Catalog\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cb\u003e:\u003c/b\u003e A catalog of publicly available geoscience datasets, products, experiments, and workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"2o884\"\u003e\u003cb\u003eJAXA (Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency)\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp data-block-key=\"evtup\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://gportal.jaxa.jp/gpr/index/index?utm_source=%27chatgpt.com%27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-external \u003e\u003cb\u003eJAXA G-Portal\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cb\u003e:\u003c/b\u003e Provides Earth-observation products from JAXA missions, searchable by variables such as precipitation, snow, vegetation, etc.\u003c/p\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"6:[[\"$\",\"$L1f\",null,{}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"id\":\"permanent-moon-base-locations-and-mars\",\"className\":\"index-module-scss-module__LrtIIa__content-container\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L20\",null,{\"visible\":false,\"type\":\"status\"}],[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"page-meta-module-scss-module__jpMY-a__container undefined\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"container-module-scss-module__GNU7ba__row \",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L21\",null,{\"breadcrumbs\":[{\"__typename\":\"Breadcrumb\",\"name\":\"Home\",\"link\":\"/\"},{\"__typename\":\"Breadcrumb\",\"name\":\"2026 NASA Space Apps Challenge\",\"link\":\"/2026/\"},{\"__typename\":\"Breadcrumb\",\"name\":\"2026 Challenges\",\"link\":\"/2026/challenges/\"},{\"__typename\":\"Breadcrumb\",\"name\":\"Permanent-Moon-Base-Locations-and-Mars\",\"link\":\"/2026/challenges/permanent-moon-base-locations-and-mars/\"}],\"theme\":\"dark\"}],\"$undefined\"]}]}],[\"$\",\"$L10\",null,{\"theme\":\"event\",\"size\":\"lg\",\"image\":{\"__typename\":\"DefaultImage\",\"id\":\"aWQ6NDEzMjY3\",\"meta\":{\"__typename\":\"ImageMeta\",\"type\":\"images.CustomImage\",\"downloadUrl\":\"https://assets.spaceappschallenge.org/media/original_images/Permanent_Moon_Base_Locations.png\"},\"title\":\"Permanent Moon Base Locations\",\"alt\":\"A muted blue Earth with bright white clouds sets behind the cratered lunar surface.\",\"caption\":\"Permanent Moon Base Locations\",\"displaySize\":\"or\",\"rendition\":{\"__typename\":\"BaseImage\",\"url\":\"https://assets.spaceappschallenge.org/media/images/Permanent_Moon_Base_Locations.2e16d0ba.fill-785x660.png\",\"fullUrl\":\"https://assets.spaceappschallenge.org/media/images/Permanent_Moon_Base_Locations.2e16d0ba.fill-785x660.png\",\"height\":466,\"width\":555}},\"hasTabs\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L22\",null,{\"title\":\"Identify Earth Locations that Analog the Permanent Moon Base Locations and Mars\",\"size\":\"lg\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L23\",null,{\"page\":{\"__typename\":\"ChallengePage\",\"id\":\"aWQ6NTUxMjk=\",\"title\":\"Identify Earth Locations that Analog the Permanent Moon Base Locations and Mars\",\"meta\":{\"__typename\":\"ExpandedPageMeta\",\"type\":\"CHALLENGE\",\"slug\":\"permanent-moon-base-locations-and-mars\",\"firstPublishedAt\":\"2026-09-17T14:21:29.086000Z\",\"lastPublishedAt\":\"2026-11-13T03:52:04.820266Z\",\"relativeUrl\":\"/2026/challenges/permanent-moon-base-locations-and-mars/\",\"live\":true,\"parent\":{\"__typename\":\"PageParent\",\"id\":\"aWQ6NTUxMTY=\"}},\"breadcrumbs\":\"$6:1:props:children:1:0:props:children:props:children:0:props:breadcrumbs\",\"featuredImage\":\"$6:1:props:children:1:1:props:image\",\"event\":\"aWQ6NA==\",\"categories\":[{\"__typename\":\"Category\",\"id\":3,\"name\":\"Advanced\",\"slug\":\"advanced\",\"description\":null,\"priority\":1,\"color\":\"#8B0A03\"},{\"__typename\":\"Category\",\"id\":2,\"name\":\"Intermediate\",\"slug\":\"intermediate\",\"description\":null,\"priority\":2,\"color\":\"#FF580A\"},{\"__typename\":\"Category\",\"id\":1,\"name\":\"Beginner/Youth\",\"slug\":\"beginneryouth\",\"description\":null,\"priority\":3,\"color\":\"#07173F\"}],\"skills\":[\"Earth Science\",\"Planets \u0026 Moons\",\"Software\",\"Space Exploration\"],\"excerpt\":\"Selecting future landing sites and lunar base locations depends on many factors, including topography, geology, environment, and available resources, to name a few. Places on Earth that have analogous characteristics to the Moon or Mars – the Atacama Desert, Haughton Crater, Death Valley – allow mission teams to test equipment and methods before they fly; however, such terrestrial analog sites are not entirely well characterized. Your challenge is to use open Earth, Moon, and Mars data from NASA and other space agencies to identify and characterize new terrestrial analogs for future bases or landing sites, drawing on deserts, polar regions, volcanic and arid terrains, caves, and other environments that share conditions with the lunar or Martian surface, then present your findings as a map, visualization, or other tool.\",\"detailsContent\":\"$24\",\"resourcesTabContent\":\"$25\",\"teamsTabContent\":\"\"},\"url\":\"/2026/challenges/permanent-moon-base-locations-and-mars//?tab=teams\"}]}]}],\"$L26\"]]}],\"$L27\"]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"28:I[96222,[\"/_next/static/chunks/2bd45f40cc5d6573.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d04c481ca7424b28.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/b43d582805300480.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/20089cdc3431a20d.js\"],\"Challenge\"]\n29:I[96222,[\"/_next/static/chunks/2bd45f40cc5d6573.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/d04c481ca7424b28.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/b43d582805300480.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/20089cdc3431a20d.js\"],\"SiteFooter\"]\n26:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"container-module-scss-module__GNU7ba__row \",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L28\",null,{\"page\":\"$6:1:props:children:1:1:props:children:props:children:props:page\"}],\"$undefined\"]}]\n27:[\"$\",\"$L29\",null,{}]\n"])</script><div hidden id="S:1"><header class="site-header-module-scss-module__bmeb3G__container"><div class="site-header-module-scss-module__bmeb3G__header"><div class="banner-module-scss-module___Oei3G__container banner-module-scss-module___Oei3G__primary"><div class="container-module-scss-module__GNU7ba__row "><div class="banner-module-scss-module___Oei3G__container-inner"><div class="banner-module-scss-module___Oei3G__title-bar"><button class="banner-module-scss-module___Oei3G__title "><span class="banner-module-scss-module___Oei3G__icon"></span><span class="banner-module-scss-module___Oei3G__title-text">The NASA Space Apps Challenge is happening now! 🎉</span></button>